<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toxandria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toxandria was a region of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, in the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campine">Campine</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, named after the Texuandri people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a> settled there in the 4th century, and in 358 the Caesar Julian accepted their submission and allowed them to stay as federates. The area became an early base of Frankish power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Texandria, later written Toxandria or Taxandria, lay in what is now the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the area known today as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campine">Campine</a>, Kempen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. The tribal name Texandri, which may be related to it, appears as Texand(ri) on an inscription dated 100 to 225 CE and as Texuandri in the work of Pliny in the 1st century CE. The form Toxiandria occurs only once, in a 9th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the Res Gestae of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a>, while Taxandria appears five times in 9th-century sources and in later documents. Copyists' errors, or the older form falling out of use, may explain the spellings.</p><p>The name is generally thought to come from the Proto-Germanic stem *tehswō(n)-, meaning right hand or south, with a contrasting suffix, so that Texandria may be read as the land of the southerners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a>, writing around 390 CE, is the first to mention the region: after their defeat by Julian, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a> were allowed to settle at a place in Toxiandria. The name returns in sources of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Between 709 and 795 the pagus of Texandria centred on the basin of the river Dommel and its tributaries, with one cluster of settlement between Alphen and Waalre and another around Overpelt.</p><p>Through a growing network of alliances among the elite, the region expanded between 815 and 914 to cover modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a> and neighbouring parts of the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and Limburg, possibly between Oosterhout, Laakdal and Reppel. In the mid-11th century Stepelinus, a monk of Saint-Trond, placed a region called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> within Texandria, the first record of that name. From about 1225 Campania, the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campine">Campine</a>, replaced Texandria as the name of the region. Texandria survived for a time as the name of a large archdeaconry in the diocese of Liège, until Campania replaced it there too by the end of the 14th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Texandria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In <b>Toxandria</b>, the Salian Franks farmed land between the Meuse and the Scheldt under Roman authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 358 the Caesar Julian forced the submission of those who had settled in <b>Toxandria</b>, in the modern Kempen, and let them remain as allies of the Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a></li><li><blockquote>In 358 the future emperor Julian accepted the submission of the Salian Franks, who had already settled in <b>Toxandria</b>, and let them stay as Roman allies, and the region became the starting point of Frankish power in the Low Countries.</blockquote><a href="/state/texuandri">Texuandri</a></li></ul></section>
